<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zahir al-Umar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1690–<a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Daher al-Omar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zahir al-Umar was an Arab sheikh of the Zaydani clan who built an autonomous domain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> in the 18th century, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> his capital and a busy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>-exporting port. He defied the Ottoman governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and allied with Ali Bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the Russian navy. He was killed in 1775 when an Ottoman fleet besieged Acre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zahir-al-umar.jpg" alt="Zahir al-Umar" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ziad Daher · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daher_el-Omar_001.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Daher al-Umar was born in 1689 or 1690. His father, Umar, was sheikh of the Zayadina, a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> origin that had settled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> in the late 17th century under Daher's grandfather, Salih; his mother came from the Sardiyya, a Bedouin tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> belonged to Sidon Eyalet, whose governors farmed out tax collection to local potentates. Around 1698 Umar became a multazim, or tax farmer, and by 1703 the French vice-consul of Sidon called him the paramount sheikh of the Galilee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 1730, with the support of the Saqr Bedouins, Daher took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and ousted its subgovernor. By about 1740 he held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>, and his fortified towns withstood sieges by Sulayman Pasha al-Azm, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in 1742–1743. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> by force in 1744, was formally made its multazim in July 1746 and moved his headquarters there around 1750. Under an agreement with the French vice-consul Joseph Blanc, the French covered his taxes and supplied firearms in return for exclusive rights to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and wheat.</p><p>In 1768 the imperial government recognised him as Sheikh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and of all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>. When Uthman Pasha al-Kurji of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> moved against him in 1770, Daher turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-bey-al-kabir">Ali Bey al-Kabir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the Shia clans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-amil">Jabal Amil</a>. In 1771 their campaigns drove Uthman Pasha and his son from Damascus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, and Daher won at Lake Hula and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and Sidon. On 6 July 1772, at his request, Russian ships under Count Orlov bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His last years were troubled by his sons, who fought one another for the succession. In March 1775 Abu al-Dhahab, who had toppled Ali Bey, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, executed the men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and drove Daher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, but he died soon after and Daher returned. In August an Ottoman flotilla under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-pasha-of-baghdad">Hasan Pasha</a> al-Jaza'iri came before Acre to collect his tax arrears, and Daher was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezzar-pasha">Ahmad Pasha al-Jazzar</a> became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and suppressed the last of the Zayadina in 1776.</p><p>Daher's security, flexible taxation and invitation to Christian and Jewish immigrants brought growth to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>. Jazzar kept his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> monopoly, but when the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s supplier in the 19th century, the region suffered a sharp downturn. Daher is regarded as a national hero among Palestinians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daher al-Umar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Zahir al-Umar</b> of the Zaydani clan was a tax farmer in Galilee.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-zahir-al-umar">Sheikhdom of Zahir al-Umar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Died in Acre, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1745" aria-label="Acre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5449 968 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Zahir al-Umar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5899 1238h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5899 1238h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1745">What was here in 1745? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
